{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And he said.</b> By reason it follows that the staff was in his hand, even though it is not written." "<b>And one could feel the darkness.</b> Yefet said that וימש חשך is like וימיש, and the meaning is that all the darkness they had departed and another came after it, thicker than it. And some say that it is deficient alef, and the word אמש in the sacred language means the night that passed, like אמש אמר אלי (Genesis 31:29), and in Arabic 'אמס,' for shin interchanges with samekh in most words, like שמש / שמס. But there is no sense to their interpretation. In my opinion, וימש is from the root of ימשש (Deut. 28), and there is no objection because it is of the doubled-root verbs, for like it in this matter is ידיהם ולא ימישון (Psalms 115:7). And שב על מצרים, for מצרים is singular, like ויאמר מצרים אנוסה (below, 14:25). Now the meaning is that they would feel the darkness with their hand, because it would be so thick that the light of fire would not burn, nor a lamp. And the proof: לא ראו איש את אחיו — neither by the light of day nor by the light of lamps."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {1 "Resolved 'יפת' as Yefet (Ibn Janāḥ), by inference from Ibn Ezra's pattern of citing Hebrew lexicographers; alternatively the Karaite exegete Japheth ben Ali."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:33:19.064219094Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}